					<p class="body1" align="left"><span class="body1 style73"> <h2>News archive</h2><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2006-12-12 | Tuesday</font></strong><br><br>so here we are in Albuquerque.&nbsp; the &quot;Mexico&quot; part of &quot;New Mexico&quot; is somewhat misleading to me in terms of weather right now.<br />
<br />
it is cold as S$*#! <br />
<br />
or maybe i&#39;m just a foo for thinkin that Mexico is supposed to be warm.&nbsp; yea, that&#39;s probably it.<br />
<br />
after visiting the Baha&#39;i Center and catching up with reknown violinist
Farzhad (we rocked a show together at the Premier a while back) RA and
I decided to kill some time yesterday evening on our day off by seeing
Apocalypto.&nbsp; <br />
<br />
still dunno what to think.&nbsp; it wasn&#39;t long ago that &quot;Yucatec Maya&quot; was
a forbidden language like most indigenous tounges of the North American
continent during the age of boarding schools and re-education
programs.&nbsp; now it&#39;s featured in the newest Mel Gibson film and is
possibly about to be the new hawtschitt like Aramaic was when the
Passion of Christ dropped.<br />
<br />
either way, i thought the film was a good watch.&nbsp; RA got a little
bored.&nbsp; this afternoon before soundcheck, we&#39;re gonna go check out
Babel.<br />
<br />
if you happen to be in the 505 this evening, come check out the 2K7
tour at the Sunshine Theater on Central Ave.&nbsp; holla holla holla.<br />
<br />
saba<p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2006-12-10 | Sunday</font></strong><br><br>Unbelievable. Wrote a big ol blog. Browser crashed. Lost it all. Back to square one...<br /><br />Perhaps it&#39;s only fitting that brutal former Chilean dictator <strong><a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augusto_Pinochet" target="_blank">Augusto Pinochet</a> </strong>died today on <strong><a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_Rights_Day" target="_blank">December 10 - International Human Rights Da</a>y</strong>. Folks will be gathering downtown Seattle this afternoon to commemorate this historic day from the convention center (7th &amp; Pike) to Westlake Plaza starting at 1PM. Maybe we&#39;ll run into <strong>Andre 3000</strong> or <strong>Charlize Theron</strong>, who are in town filming for <em>The Battle in Seattle</em>.<br /><br />For all you podcast-downloaders out there, <strong>Blue Scholars</strong> is featured at <a href="http://www.SoundSceneRevolution.com">SoundSceneRevolution.com</a>. Check the audio interview we did backstage in San Francisco (thanks, Rich).<br /><br />In other miscellaneous news, the <a href="http://www.bluescholars.com/artistedition/"><strong>Peekaboo! limited edition shirts are going fast</strong></a>. Get you one for the holiday rush. <br /><br />Calling all 206-area hip-hop heads. Don&#39;t forget to circle Thursday, <strong>DECEMBER 14</strong> on your calendar. That&#39;s the night the first installation of <strong>Dope Emporium</strong> goes down at the <strong>Capitol Hill Arts Center</strong>. It&#39;ll be a gathering of the heavy hitters of the bubbling 206 hip-hop scene including a wide spectrum of folks from <strong>Sport-N-Life Records</strong>, <strong>Oldominion</strong>, <strong>Grynch</strong>, as well as MassLine affiliates <strong>Geologic</strong> (rockin a solo set) and <strong>Abyssinian Creole</strong> plus many more. Oh, did we mention that the show is <strong>ALL -AGES AND FREE</strong>? Big shoutout to <strong>Silent Lambs Project</strong>, <strong>The Stranger</strong> and <strong>KBCS 91.3FM</strong> for organizing this landmark event. Also, thank you to the homie and event oragnizer <strong>Rahwa</strong> for the good eats at <a href="../smf/index.php?topic=503.0"><strong>Hidmo Eritrean Cuisine</strong></a> (on 20th &amp; Jackson!). More info and discussion on the show in <a href="../smf/index.php?topic=502.0">The Forum</a>. And peep Charles Mudede&#39;s article on the show in this weeks <a href="http://www.thestranger.com/seattle/Content?oid=115466">The Stranger</a>.<br /><br />Lastly, everybody <a href="../smf/index.php?topic=544.0">shoutout Sabzi a happy birthday</a>!<p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2006-12-10 | Sunday</font></strong><br><br><img src="http://www.akomplice-clothing.com/images/clothing/season5/5-6-1.jpg" alt="akomplice" title="akomplice" width="358" height="232" /><br /><br /><strong>aspen</strong> is an interesting place.<br /><br />i don&#39;t think i&#39;ve ever been to a place with such a high concentration of wealth.&nbsp; we met more <strong>australians</strong> and <strong>south afrikaaners</strong> at the club than any other citizens, and i heard more european languages spoken in the street than english;&nbsp; all here in the heart of colorado!&nbsp; <br /><br />some social investigation informed us that the cost of living is quite high ($1600/mo. for a studio apartment is considered &quot;a steal&quot;), everything--with the exception of the club--seems to close at about 7pm, most of the workers that labor in aspen commute from the &quot;worker town&quot; about an hour out of the city (just like the good old feudal days!), and the average ticket price of the club we played at last night is something like <strong>$300 bucks a show</strong>.&nbsp; <br /><br />when we said &quot;hey! that&#39;s expensive!&quot;&nbsp; emily, our host for the evening, said &quot;no, expensive is when we do special events and charge $1200 a head to come see the show; we&#39;ll still sell out tho.&quot;&nbsp; we&#39;re talkin about a regular old club, folks.&nbsp; like chop suey, but with stools and woodgrain.<br /><br />nevertheless, the folks in aspen were quite live.&nbsp; they showed lots of love to us and all the other players on the tour.&nbsp; we look forward to coming back.<br /><br />also got a chance to catch up with the folks that do <strong>Akomplice</strong> clothing.&nbsp; i did not know these doods were from colorado.&nbsp; they had a hard time blending in to all the ski bunnies in the club what with their extra clean all over hoodies and gold foil shirts.&nbsp; big up fellas, keep doin your thing and continue to serve up the hottschitt.<br /><br />we&#39;ll be in boulder tonight, coincidentally with your boy <strong>Macklemore</strong> who&#39;s got a show across the street from ours.&nbsp; before the show, we&#39;ll likely just sip some Beast and talk about how we can&#39;t wait to get back to Seattle.<p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2006-12-09 | Saturday</font></strong><br><br><p>what it gwan?<br /><br />Tonight at <strong>Youngstown Cultural Arts Center, TSB presents...</strong> <em>&quot;So Fresh, So Clean&quot;</em> featuring The C.R. Avery Band, <strong>Abyssinian Creole</strong>, YouthSpeaks, the Service Band, Roberto Carlos Ascalon, Jeromeskee and the ArtsCorps All-City Breakers... 7:30 pm, and thats an all-ages show ya&#39;ll<br /><br />and tomorrow, you don&#39;t want to miss the Seattle screening of the documentary <strong>Betrayal Of Democracy: Ethiopia</strong> happening at the <strong>Langston Hughes Performing Arts Center<br /></strong></p><p>This film gives an insider&#39;s view of politics in a Third World country where a system of tyranny, corruption and oppression is being held in place by a complex assortment of players, ranging from Ethiopians themselves to those in the international community, all who influence the internal affairs of the second most populous country in Africa, not always with the best interests of the Ethiopian people in mind. </p><br /><p>In an atmosphere where information is tightly controlled, now you can discover what is actually going on behind the scenes as the people of Ethiopia speak out from the dark places of oppression, bringing the lens of truth to the public about the rampant human rights abuses that go on in the secret places of this regime while captivating the world with an illusion of democracy. </p><p><br />There will be 3 showings, 11:30 am, 1:30 am, and 3:00 pm and tickets are $15.&nbsp; More information can be found at <a href="http://www.anuakjustice.org/"><strong>http://www.anuakjustice.org</strong></a></p><p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2006-12-09 | Saturday</font></strong><br><br><img src="http://www.seattleschools.org/area/facilities/BEXII/Maple.jpg" alt="" width="366" height="251" /><br /><br />give it up for press.... ahem.<br /><br />Common Market has been dubbed <strong>Artist of the Day</strong> on <strong>Spin.com</strong>.  if you click <a href="http://www.spin.com/"><u><strong>here</strong></u></a> and scroll down past the picture of Ron Artest at the mic you can peep the article--er, blurb--that&#39;s been mentioned.<br /><br />our dood <strong>Gabriel Teodros</strong> has been garnering media attention as well, what with a review in <strong>Jive</strong> and <strong>Vice</strong>.  if you catch <strong>geeteezy</strong> on the street, ask him what it&#39;s like to suddenly be approached by a bunch of writers simply because he put together such a filthy album.  he&#39;ll smile briefly and express love and respect for the folks who are taking notice of the art, but then he&#39;ll likely explain that <strong>it&#39;s wack to give one individual a bunch of attention</strong> for any reason.  then he&#39;ll put on his hood throw up the <strong>N-Dub</strong> and take another sip of <strong>The Beast</strong>.  <br /><br />of course, that&#39;s just my speculation of how the discourse would go down.<br /><br />we stay steady on the grind out here on the road as we pass through Park City, Utah, and make way for Colorado.  if you happen to be in Aspen or Boulder in the next couple days, come holla at us!<br /><br />saba<p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><center><div class="pagenumbers"><A HREF="archive.php?from=255">[&lt;&lt;]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=0">[1]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=5">[2]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=10">[3]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=15">[4]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=20">[5]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=25">[6]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=30">[7]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=35">[8]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=40">[9]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=45">[10]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=50">[11]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=55">[12]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=60">[13]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=65">[14]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=70">[15]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=75">[16]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=80">[17]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=85">[18]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=90">[19]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=95">[20]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=100">[21]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=105">[22]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=110">[23]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=115">[24]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=120">[25]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=125">[26]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=130">[27]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=135">[28]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=140">[29]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=145">[30]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?fr